window.DOCS_INDEX=[{"p":"Getting Started","h":"Getting Started","u":"getting-started.html#getting-started","x":""},{"p":"Getting Started","h":"Installing","u":"getting-started.html#installing","x":"Import the package. Everything lands in one folder, Assets/AllInOnePrefabBuilderKit/ . There is nothing else to set up: no packages to install, no registration, no project settings changed. Unity 6000.3 or newer (tested on 6000.3 and 6000.6). Built-in Render Pipeline or URP. (HDRP isn't tested yet; the sign text shader has an HDRP pass, but it's untried.) Works with Enter Play Mode Options (domain reload off)."},{"p":"Getting Started","h":"Where your data lives","u":"getting-started.html#where-your-data-lives","x":"The Prefab Builder never writes into its own folder. Everything you make — kits, styles, rule sets, character profiles, palettes, and the placeable copies of pack pieces — goes into a data folder : Assets/AllInOnePrefabBuilderData/ by default (the dataFolder project setting). It is created the first time something is saved there. Updating the Prefab Builder replaces only its own folder, so your data is never overwritten. Project-wide settings (layer and tag names, surface names, scene folders, pack detection) are stored in ProjectSettings/AllInOneWorldBuilderSettings.asset , so they are shared"},{"p":"Getting Started","h":"The menu","u":"getting-started.html#the-menu","x":"Everything is under Tools > All In One Prefab Builder : Item What it does Prefab Builder Opens the Prefab Builder window (see The Prefab Builder Window ). Everything else is reachable from it. Add Pack... Opens the Add Pack wizard (see Adding Packs ). Presets (Export, Import)... Saves or loads rule sets, palettes, kit annotations and settings (see Presets )."},{"p":"Getting Started","h":"First use","u":"getting-started.html#first-use","x":"The first time the Prefab Builder needs a rule set, it makes the defaults in your data folder: Default Rule Set — placement rules based on common city-building practice. Standard Human — a Character Profile for a 1.8 m adult. Warm Town and Clean Suburb — two palettes. All of them are yours to change; see Rules, Profiles, Palettes and Styles ."},{"p":"Getting Started","h":"The first-run tour","u":"getting-started.html#the-first-run-tour","x":"The first time you open the Prefab Builder (with no kits yet), a small card at the top of the Place tab takes you through it, one step at a time with Back and Next : add a pack, pick a piece, place it, snapping and the grid, the other ways to place, Dress and Fix, and where help is. The first three show a tick once you've done them. Skip hides it; ⋮ > Show the tour again starts it over."},{"p":"Getting Started","h":"The sample kits","u":"getting-started.html#the-sample-kits","x":"The Prefab Builder comes with sample models to try everything on before you add a pack of your own, all Kenney's and CC0 (free for any use): the City Kits (Commercial, Industrial, Roads, Suburban) in Samples/Kenney City Kit , and Modular Buildings in Samples/Kenney Modular Buildings . Click Try the sample kits in the empty palette, Use the sample kits in the tour, or ⋮ > Add the sample kits , and they become two kits: Kenney City (sample) — roads, shops, houses, factories, trees, fences, street lights, cones and barriers, sorted into categories, at one scale (×10: 10 m road tiles) on a 10 m gr"},{"p":"Getting Started","h":"The demo scenes","u":"getting-started.html#the-demo-scenes","x":"Samples/Showcase/Showcase City.unity is a little city of the sample kits: drawn roads, a downtown block, a block of houses, a block of buildings stacked from the modular blocks (hollow: try the cutaway on them), a fenced park, and street lights along every road. Samples/Showcase/Try It.unity is half built, to try the tools on: a road to carry on (Roads), an empty block to stamp, brush and fill in, trees to fence (Fence), containers to drop, settle and untangle (Physics). Each has a Camera spots object: select one of its children and use GameObject > Align View to Selected to look where it look"},{"p":"The Prefab Builder Window","h":"The Prefab Builder Window","u":"window.html#the-prefab-builder-window","x":"Open it with Tools > All In One Prefab Builder > Prefab Builder (or Alt+Shift+W )."},{"p":"The Prefab Builder Window","h":"Layout","u":"window.html#layout","x":"Tabs: Move, Place, Physics, Dress, Fix, Check and Settings. One tab is visible at a time. When the window is too narrow for them, the tabs scroll sideways — the mouse wheel over them, or the arrows at their ends — as the mode selector and every row of tabs in a tool do. Mode selector: under the tabs. Each tab has a few modes; only the chosen mode's panel is shown. Each mode button has its number before its name (\"1 Stamp\": press 1 over the Scene view or the window); they share the row evenly, as the tabs do; drag a tab or a tool along its bar to put it in another place — the number keys follow"},{"p":"The Prefab Builder Window","h":"The Scene overlay","u":"window.html#the-scene-overlay","x":"The Prefab Builder overlay in the Scene view is kept small: the mode and its tab, the mode's most used settings — the very same settings as in its panel (change one in either and the other shows it at once) — and one grey line saying what the next click does: Mode In the overlay Stamp Snap to pieces, Place over red Brush Radius, Density, Stay on this floor, Place over red Row Spacing, Place over red, Snap to pieces Spline Fronts face, Spacing, Curved, Place over red, Snap to pieces; Lay it / Loop / Clear while drawing Fill Area / Selection / Inverse (with Margin, Outside / Inside, Round it, an"},{"p":"The Prefab Builder Window","h":"Messages","u":"window.html#messages","x":"What the Prefab Builder tells you — \"Row of 12\", \"3 piece(s) filed\", \"Pick the pieces first\" — shows as a box across the top of the Scene view , in bold under a small Prefab Builder title (blue; amber with Heads up when there's something to fix), and fades after a few seconds (longer messages stay longer). A new message replaces the last. It never takes a click: you can keep working under it."},{"p":"The Prefab Builder Window","h":"Unity's own tools","u":"window.html#unitys-own-tools","x":"While a mode that places things is active, the Prefab Builder takes the Scene view's clicks and Unity's transform tool is set aside. To select, move, rotate or scale things as usual — with the window still open — press Q, W, E or Y, or click a tool in Unity's toolbar , or press Esc when there's nothing to cancel. The Prefab Builder then leaves the Scene view alone (the overlay says Unity tools ). Click anywhere in the Prefab Builder window — or Back to … in the overlay — and the tool you were on is back, as it was; picking a piece, a mode or a tab does it too. (R and T stay the Prefab Builder'"},{"p":"The Prefab Builder Window","h":"What the tools aim at","u":"window.html#what-the-tools-aim-at","x":"Every tool lands on whatever is under the mouse: anything with a collider, and models without one too — a pack's models placed straight in (as in Demo City), roofs, roads, tables — by their meshes. (The Prefab Builder keeps hidden stand-ins of those meshes to aim at; nothing is added to the models themselves.) Scatter such as grass is aimed through."},{"p":"The Prefab Builder Window","h":"Tabs in this version","u":"window.html#tabs-in-this-version","x":"Tab Modes Move Move, Rotate, Scale, Select, Show / Hide Place Stamp, Erase, Brush, Fill, Row, Spline, Roads Physics Place, Scatter, Arrange Dress Street, Closures, Signs, Decals, Cables, Fence, Vary Fix Ground, Floating, Doubles, Select, Replace, Randomize Settings Kits, Styles, Rules, Palettes, Folders, Floors & Terrain, Presets, Hotkeys Check Clipping, Validate, Play, Measure, Performance"},{"p":"The Prefab Builder Window","h":"Undo","u":"window.html#undo","x":"Every action is one Undo step: a placed piece (with the folders made for it), a snap, a drop to the ground, a Fix all."},{"p":"Adding Packs","h":"Adding Packs","u":"adding-packs.html#adding-packs","x":"A kit is a pack turned into placeable pieces: every prefab or model measured, sorted into a category and annotated (front side, corner piece, surface…). The Add Pack wizard makes kits. Open it with Tools > All In One Prefab Builder > Add Pack..."},{"p":"Adding Packs","h":"1. Choose the pack","u":"adding-packs.html#1-choose-the-pack","x":"The first page offers four ways in. None of them changes anything until you click. Found in your project. Folders that look like packs (at least 5 prefabs or models, not a kit yet) are listed. Click Scan to review one, or Not a pack to never be offered it again. Publisher folders holding several packs ( Assets/Publisher/PackA , .../PackB ) are offered as separate packs. Add from disk (.zip or folder). For packs that aren't a .unitypackage — most free kits come as a .zip . Click Choose a pack folder, or the folder your .zip files are in and pick either a pack's folder or the folder holding its "},{"p":"Adding Packs","h":"2. What the scanner does","u":"adding-packs.html#2-what-the-scanner-does","x":"Finds every prefab in the folder, and every model (FBX, OBJ, …) that none of those prefabs already uses. A pack that ships only models works; a pack that ships prefabs of its models isn't counted twice. Measures each piece without placing it: visible box (footprint and height), triangles (LOD 0 only), materials, and where its pivot is (bottom centre, centre, a corner, or elsewhere). Flags problems : an empty material slot, a shader for a different render pipeline (it would look pink), a scaled root, an animated character, a piece with nothing visible. Pieces with nothing visible and animated c"},{"p":"Adding Packs","h":"3. The review screen","u":"adding-packs.html#3-the-review-screen","x":"Pack check (at the top, when there's anything to say): what the scan found across the whole pack, counted — materials made for another render pipeline (they look pink until converted), broken shaders, empty material slots, pieces with no mesh, scaled roots, odd pivots, animated characters, categories guessed from shape only. Show them shows just those pieces; in a URP project, Render Pipeline Converter… opens Unity's own converter (it changes the pack's materials — the kit never does). Kit name and Style. A kit belongs to a style (a look, such as \"low poly town\"); a new style is made for the f"},{"p":"Adding Packs","h":"Placeable copies","u":"adding-packs.html#placeable-copies","x":"With Make placeable copies on, each piece gets a copy in Kits/<Kit name> Prefabs/ : a prefab variant of the pack's prefab or model (so a pack update still flows through) with what a world piece needs, as its category's rules say: a box collider fitted to it if it has no collider, static flags (batching, occlusion, reflection probes) on categories that don't move, a Surface Tag carrying its surface on ground and road pieces. With a kit scale other than 1, the copy is a scale-1 object holding the scaled original, so its collider is never scaled. The pack's own files are never changed. Copies are"},{"p":"Adding Packs","h":"Re-scanning and managing kits","u":"adding-packs.html#re-scanning-and-managing-kits","x":"In the Prefab Builder window, Settings > Kits (or the Inspector of a kit asset): Re-scan and review — scans the pack folders again (for a pack update, or new pieces). Everything you set by hand is kept; guesses are refreshed; pieces whose files are gone are dropped. Rename — the kit and its copies' folder (the copies keep their links). Hidden — keep the kit but hide it from the palette. Merge another kit into this one — its pieces and folders move here; the other kit is deleted. Export its annotations — a preset file with only this kit (see Presets ). Remove kit — deletes the kit, and optional"},{"p":"Move Tab","h":"Move Tab","u":"move-tab.html#move-tab","x":"Move, rotate and scale what's already placed — with the kit's snapping instead of Unity's handles. The Move tab comes first ( Shift+1 over the Scene view picks it; M goes straight to Move and S to Select). Select objects the usual way (click, Shift-click, drag a box); the handles work on the top objects of the selection (a child moves with its parent). In Prefab Mode they work inside the prefab. 1 to 5 switch between Move , Rotate , Scale , Select and Show / Hide while the mouse is over the Scene view — only in this tab, so they never get in the way elsewhere (change them in Settings > Hotkeys"},{"p":"Move Tab","h":"The anchor","u":"move-tab.html#the-anchor","x":"Every tool works from the anchor (the large yellow dot). Choose it with the two buttons in the panel (and the Scene overlay): Pivot : the (first) object's own pivot. No dots. Point (to begin with, the bottom middle): dots show on the selection's box — its corners, the middles of its edges and faces, and its centre; they grow under the mouse. Click a dot to make it the anchor; Move's arrows, Rotate's rings and Scale's square then sit there. From a point: Move puts the anchor on the grid (with the grid on): pick a corner to line corners up. Along an axis where the anchor is a middle, the box's e"},{"p":"Move Tab","h":"Move","u":"move-tab.html#move","x":"Drag the selected object itself to slide it over the scene onto whatever is under the mouse — the ground, a roof, a table, a shelf (models without colliders too). Or drag an arrow to move the selection along X, Y or Z, or a coloured square to move it along two axes at once. Land on what's under it (on by default): moved along X or Z, what stands on something keeps standing on what's below it as it goes — up onto a step, down off a table. A sign on a wall or a hanging lamp keeps its height, and the Y arrow always lifts freely. With Snap to pieces on, the selection snaps flush against the pieces"},{"p":"Move Tab","h":"Rotate","u":"move-tab.html#rotate","x":"Drag a ring to turn the selection through the anchor, in steps of Turn step (the rule set's rotation step unless set here): the green ring turns it round the up axis, the red one round X and the blue one round Z (tipping it over). R and Shift+R — or Turn right / Turn left — turn it round the up axis a step at a time. T and Shift+T tilt each selected object a Turn step — its front down, or up — on its own foot, lifted so nothing sinks into the ground. Turn each at random : every selected object turned round its own middle by a random number of steps — variety for a row of houses or crates in on"},{"p":"Move Tab","h":"Scale","u":"move-tab.html#scale","x":"A box shows round the selection. Drag a face to stretch the selection that way; with the grid on, the face lands on the grid (the top on the height step). Hold Alt to stretch both sides at once. Objects turned by quarter turns stretch exactly along their own sides. Drag a corner (the pink balls) or the square at the anchor to scale evenly, in steps of Scale step (0.1: 10 % at a time), round the anchor — a corner that is itself the anchor scales round the opposite corner. Click a corner without dragging to make it the anchor. Reset scale : each selected object back at the scale it has in its pr"},{"p":"Move Tab","h":"Select","u":"move-tab.html#select","x":"Select placed pieces by a shape you draw in the Scene view — handy before moving, scattering or arranging them: Box — drag its base on the ground, then move the mouse up for its height and click: everything whose middle is inside it (a low box picks the ground floor only). Click straight away, or double-click, and it stays flat: everything standing in it, at any height. Sphere — drag from the middle out: everything whose middle is inside the ball. Circle — drag a circle on the ground from its middle out: everything standing in it, at any height. On screen — press Space (or the button): everyth"},{"p":"Move Tab","h":"Show / Hide","u":"move-tab.html#show--hide","x":""},{"p":"Move Tab","h":"The cutaway","u":"move-tab.html#the-cutaway","x":"To see and build inside, slice the top or the side away. The Scene view stops drawing everything past the cut — through the middle of a building, even one made as a single mesh. The switches are at the top of this panel and on the Cutaway line of the Scene overlay, in every tool, so you can turn them on while placing: Top ( C ) slices away everything above a height: roofs, ceilings and the floors above. A blue sheet shows where the cut is; drag its blue arrow up or down to put it anywhere (or type the height in this panel). The first time, it starts a storey above the middle of the Scene view."},{"p":"Move Tab","h":"Categories","u":"move-tab.html#categories","x":"Hide or lock every placed piece of a category while you work — all the roads, the buildings, the props: Hide takes them out of the Scene view, and every tool aims straight through them: hide the roofs and place furniture inside, hide the props and fix the road under them. Lock leaves them showing but they can't be clicked or selected — still there to place on. Only shows just that category and hides the rest. Show all and Unlock all bring everything back. A category's switch marked * is on for some of its pieces only; a click sets it for all of them. Scatter on the small-props layer has a grou"},{"p":"Place Tab","h":"Place Tab","u":"place-tab.html#place-tab","x":"Every Place mode shares the palette below its controls. Roads drawn with Place > Roads in the Try It sample scene."},{"p":"Place Tab","h":"The palette","u":"place-tab.html#the-palette","x":"Every piece of every visible kit, as a thumbnail — favourites first. Drag a piece to another place in the palette to put it there (your own order comes first; the Prefab Builder's ⋮ menu puts it back); dragged into the Scene view, it's placed. Search by name; filter by kit and category ; ★ shows favourites only; the slider sizes the thumbnails (about: they always grow to fill the width of the window). Recent: the pieces you used last, in a strip above the grid. Click a piece to make it the active piece (the Place tab opens if it wasn't showing). Drag a piece into the Scene view: while you drag"},{"p":"Place Tab","h":"What's selected","u":"place-tab.html#whats-selected","x":"Select something in the Scene view (or a prefab in the Project window) and a bar above the palette shows it: A kit piece: it's framed in orange in the palette, which scrolls to it. Pick up uses it — the same as clicking it in the palette (the piece to stamp, or on the brush or the tool's list); Rename… renames it (and what's placed of it); ⋮ opens its menu (category, floor type, scale…). Something that isn't in a kit yet (a prefab or model, or an object in the scene): Add to kit ▾ — pick a kit (or My Pieces , a kit of your own) and a category (or a new one), and it's a piece in one go. A prefa"},{"p":"Place Tab","h":"Scaling a piece","u":"place-tab.html#scaling-a-piece","x":"Right-click a piece in the palette > Scale : 50 % to 200 %, or Custom… for a slider above the palette. It's the piece's own size on top of its kit's scale, kept through re-scans. Its placeable copy is remade at that size, so every copy already placed changes with it — and the ghost, snapping and every tool use the new size. (It needs placeable copies, on by default: Settings > Kits.)"},{"p":"Place Tab","h":"Quick pick","u":"place-tab.html#quick-pick","x":"Press Alt+Shift+Q anywhere — or Quick pick next to the palette's search — and a search box opens at the mouse. Type part of a piece's name — or its category or kit; every word counts, in any order — and press Enter : it's picked as if clicked in the palette (Stamp's piece, or added to the Brush, Row, Spline, Fill or Fence list; with no placing tool open, Stamp). Up and Down choose, Esc closes it. Names starting with what you typed come first. (Change the key in Edit > Shortcuts — Space, say.)"},{"p":"Place Tab","h":"Saved sets","u":"place-tab.html#saved-sets","x":"Select a few placed pieces — a bus stop's bench, sign and bin — and the bar above the palette offers Save as set... : name it and press Enter. The set joins the My Sets kit and shows in the palette like any piece: stamp it, drag it in, turn it with R — it snaps and turns as one, and Fill, Row and the Brush can use it too. It's a prefab in your data folder ( Added Pieces/My Sets ) holding the pieces exactly as they stood — turned, sized and with their overrides (a sign's text, a material) — its pivot at the middle of their bottom, filed in the category of the biggest of them. The pieces you sel"},{"p":"Place Tab","h":"Stamp","u":"place-tab.html#stamp","x":"Places the active piece where you click. Pick a piece in the palette — or Alt-click a placed piece in the Scene view to pick it up (the eyedropper; an Alt-drag still orbits the camera). Move the mouse in the Scene view: a see-through ghost follows it over whatever is underneath. - Green: it fits. Red: it overlaps something (more than touching). - The yellow arrow points to the piece's front (set on the review screen). Click to place it. Keep clicking to place more. Where the ghost is red, Shift-click places it anyway — or switch on Place over red (Stamp panel, Over red in the overlay, or O whi"},{"p":"Place Tab","h":"Snap to pieces and the grid","u":"place-tab.html#snap-to-pieces-and-the-grid","x":"Two separate switches — use either, both or neither: Snap to pieces treats pieces like Lego. Bring a piece near another — within the snap distance (0.35 m by default; the slider under Snap to pieces in the Stamp panel: bigger grabs from further away) — and it snaps flush against it (side by side, or sitting on top), then lines up with it along that side as well as it can (its ends or middle level with the other's). Flat tiles work the same way: bring a road up to a road and it lies right beside it, lined up — even when part of it is over the other road's edge, it's pushed off to lie beside it "},{"p":"Place Tab","h":"Changing the grid","u":"place-tab.html#changing-the-grid","x":"The grid is the same for every tool that places things — Stamp, Brush, Row, Spline, Fill, Fence and Signs — so its Grid row is only in the Scene overlay (the Prefab Builder popup), in the same place for all of them: the last line, over the grey hint. It shows the grid of the piece in hand (Stamp's piece, the first picked piece, the sign board), and every tool follows it: Stamp's pieces and Row's first piece put their box edge on it (a piece narrower than the grid goes in the middle of the cell under the mouse), Spline's points and Fence's and Fill's corners go on it, Fill's spread pieces and t"},{"p":"Place Tab","h":"Brush","u":"place-tab.html#brush","x":"Paints many pieces at once: grass, litter, rocks, trees. Click pieces in the palette to put them on the brush (click again to take one off). Each gets a weight (how often it comes up; 0 = never) in Multiple. With a Grouping picked, small pieces go down as scatter into it (and the rest whole, into it too); with none, every piece is placed whole, in its folder. Drag in the Scene view. The circle fills up to the density (pieces per 10 × 10 m); each piece is turned and scaled at random ( More : random turn, random scale) and stood on whatever is under it. Pieces never crowd each other, and paintin"},{"p":"Place Tab","h":"Scatter","u":"place-tab.html#scatter","x":"A piece small enough for the rule set's scatter size (1 m tall, 1.5 m wide by default) goes down as a scatter copy : a prefab variant in your data folder with no colliders, no shadows, on the small-props layer (Settings > Folders; if the layer isn't in the project you're asked once whether to add it). Scatter is filed under World/Scatter (source)/<group> . Grouping (\"Grass\", \"Litter\"…): the group every tool shares (see Groupings ); scatter needs one — with none picked, the brush paints whole pieces. Each group combines on its own."},{"p":"Place Tab","h":"Combine Scatter","u":"place-tab.html#combine-scatter","x":"Combine merges the current group's scatter into a few meshes — one per chunk (the rule set's chunk size, 50 m by default), one part per material — under World/Scatter/<group> . Thousands of objects become a handful. The originals stay in their folder, switched off and tagged EditorOnly (left out of builds). Un-combine takes the chunks away and switches the originals back on — to paint or erase more, then combine again. Combining again only rebuilds chunks whose pieces changed. Chunk meshes are files in your data folder ( Combined Scatter/<scene>/<group>/ ). ⋮ : combine or un-combine every grou"},{"p":"Place Tab","h":"Picking pieces for Brush, Row, Spline, Fill and Fence","u":"place-tab.html#picking-pieces-for-brush-row-spline-fill-and-fence","x":"These modes use the palette to pick their pieces. Pick: Single / Multiple at the top of each mode's panel: Single — one piece at a time: a palette click swaps the picked piece for the one clicked. Multiple — several pieces: a palette click adds the piece, or takes it out if it's already picked. Alt-click a placed piece in the Scene view to pick it, just as a palette click would — in every tool with a palette (Stamp, Brush, Row, Spline, Fill, Fence, Erase, the Dress tools and Replace). An Alt-drag still orbits the camera. Or press Alt+Shift+Q and type its name ( Quick pick ). Picked pieces are "},{"p":"Place Tab","h":"Row","u":"place-tab.html#row","x":"Pieces in a straight line: walls, fences, hedges, facades, a line of trees. Pick pieces in the palette (several are used in turn — or at random: More > Random order , shown once more than one piece is picked — mostly random: never three of the same in a row; New mix picks again). For the ends (a post, a corner, an end wall), switch to Multiple and set a piece's dropdown to Start cap or End cap . Before you press it works like Stamp: a ghost of the first piece sits under the mouse, snapped like a stamped piece (flush against a neighbour, else the grid), R turns it a Turn step (hold R and move t"},{"p":"Place Tab","h":"Spline","u":"place-tab.html#spline","x":"A line through any number of points, curved or straight: fences, walls, hedges, lights or road pieces along a bend. It uses the same pieces, caps and settings as Row (its own copy of them) — in the same order. Its More has Count and Gapped to fit too — with both, exactly that many pieces spaced evenly from the spline's first point to its last, equal gaps between them (a closed spline: all the way round, the last as far from the first as the others) — and Curved . Pick the pieces in the palette. Click points in the Scene view. A curve runs through them with the pieces laid along it, each turned"},{"p":"Place Tab","h":"Fill","u":"place-tab.html#fill","x":"Like the brush, over an area: pick pieces, choose what to fill, and it's filled with them. What it fills — the row of buttons at the top of its settings (and in the Scene overlay): Area : the rectangle you drag. Selection : the outline of what's selected — click things in the Scene view (Shift or Ctrl adds more); an L-shaped lot stays an L, things apart are each filled — Margin metres past its edges (0: exactly its outline; negative: that far inside them). The yellow outline and the ghosts show it; Fill the selection fills it. Inverse : round the selection, leaving it out — around buildings, n"},{"p":"Place Tab","h":"Erase","u":"place-tab.html#erase","x":"Drag the red circle over the Scene view: the kit pieces under it are removed ( 2 in the Place tab picks it). Nothing picked: every kit piece under the circle. Pick pieces in the palette to erase only those (Single or Multiple, as in the other tools). Category erases only pieces of one category ( Any : every category). Leave floors (on by default) never erases ground and road pieces — clearing the litter off a street never takes the street with it. A piece is under the circle when its middle is inside it, or when the circle is on its footprint — aim at a roof and the building goes. Pieces on a "},{"p":"Place Tab","h":"Roads","u":"place-tab.html#roads","x":"Draw a road and the right tiles go down — straights, corners, T-junctions, crossroads and dead ends from your kit, each turned the right way ( 7 in the Place tab picks it). Drag across the ground : the road follows the mouse a cell at a time (the cell under the mouse is outlined; the road so far shows as you draw it). Drag back over it to undraw; Esc stops. Let go and it's laid — one Undo step. Drawn round a block, ending in the cell beside where it began, it closes up: a loop all the way round. It joins the roads already there : start on a road, end on one or cross one, and the tile there bec"},{"p":"Place Tab","h":"Re-roll and Select what was just placed","u":"place-tab.html#re-roll-and-select-what-was-just-placed","x":"Not happy with the mix? After a Fill , a Brush stroke, a Row , a Spline or a Physics Place drop, press N (or Re-roll in the Scene overlay) to lay it again in the same place with a new random mix — one Undo step, and press it again for another. Shift+N (or Select ) selects what was just placed, to move it, file it or delete it. Fill lays the very same fill again (same area, pieces and settings). Brush paints the same stroke again — the same dabs — with the brush's pieces and settings as they are then, so you can change the density and re-roll. Row and Spline pick their pieces again in a new ran"},{"p":"Place Tab","h":"Building prefabs (Prefab Mode)","u":"place-tab.html#building-prefabs-prefab-mode","x":"Every tool works inside Prefab Mode too: open a prefab (double-click it in the Project window, or its arrow in the Hierarchy) and Stamp, Brush, Row, Spline, Fill, Fence, Erase, the Dress tools and the Fix tools build inside it — a building, a furnished room, a street corner — saved with the prefab and reusable anywhere. What's placed goes under the prefab's root (in its category folders there, or the group), never into the scene behind it. The tools aim at the prefab's own ground and snap to its own pieces; the scene behind (Prefab Mode's \"In Context\") is only for looking at — it's never snapp"},{"p":"Place Tab","h":"Groupings (every tool)","u":"place-tab.html#groupings-every-tool","x":"One grouping for every tool. Under the settings of Stamp, Brush, Row, Spline, Fill and Fence — and Dress > Closures — Grouping picks the group what you place goes into (World/Scatter (source)/ group ; pick one, or type a name and click New ). It's the same setting everywhere: pick \"Market\" in Stamp and what you brush, lay, fill, fence or close goes into \"Market\" too, so it all combines together. Combine merges the group into a few meshes — one per chunk and material — and Un-combine brings the pieces back to change them. Combining keeps the pieces' colliders (copied into the combined group), s"},{"p":"Place Tab","h":"Scrolling","u":"place-tab.html#scrolling","x":"The picked pieces and the settings scroll on their own: the pick list past three pieces, the settings past eight lines (or half the Prefab Builder window, if that's less). Settings that grow past that — More opened, say — are hidden below straight away: scroll down for them; the palette never jumps. The mouse wheel scrolls whatever it's over — the pick list, the settings or the palette — so the palette always keeps its room."},{"p":"Place Tab","h":"Auto-parent","u":"place-tab.html#auto-parent","x":"A placed or dropped kit piece files itself under its category's scene folder: World/Buildings , World/Props … (Settings > Folders: the root and one folder per category). Missing folders are made — plain empty objects at 0,0,0, scale 1. A piece dropped into something in the Hierarchy stays where you put it. Objects with a component type listed under never re-parent (networked objects, for example) are never moved. Floor type folders: every piece with a floor type files under the Ground folder, in its floor type's folder — World/Ground/Grass , World/Ground/Wood (Settings > Folders > Floor folder"},{"p":"Physics Tab","h":"Physics Tab","u":"physics-tab.html#physics-tab","x":"Real physics for building — drop, throw and settle pieces so they land the way things really do ( Shift+3 over the Scene view picks it). The fall always happens in a hidden copy of the area round it: nothing else in your scene moves (not even objects with a Rigidbody of their own), and once settled the pieces are ordinary placed pieces, with nothing physical left on them. They land on whatever is there — the ground, a terrain, roofs, tables, shelves, walls — by their colliders, or by their meshes when they have none. Scatter such as grass is fallen through. You see them fall : each stops exact"},{"p":"Physics Tab","h":"Place","u":"physics-tab.html#place","x":"Drop pieces and let them settle — crates in a heap, rubble, rocks down a slope, books on a shelf, litter on a table. What drops: the pieces picked in the palette (Single or Multiple, with weights as in the Brush) — or tick Copies of the selection to drop copies of the selected objects (picked at random; prefab copies stay linked to their prefab). Drop: Stamp — one piece, as the Place tab's Stamp places it: its ghost shows under the mouse (at Height, where it starts to fall), R and Shift+R turn it, T and Shift+T tilt it (hold R or T and move the mouse to do it freely), and a click (or Space) dr"},{"p":"Physics Tab","h":"Scatter","u":"physics-tab.html#scatter","x":"Mess the selection up — believably. Select objects (each placed piece moves on its own; a selected folder stands for the pieces in it), pick how, and press Space (or the button): Spread — each piece is tossed to a random spot on the ground within the Radius round the selection's middle. Explode — blown outward from the selection's middle (Space), or from where you click ; Strength sets how hard (the nearest fly furthest). Heap — everything tossed to the middle, landing one after another into a pile ( Pile size : smaller makes it taller). Nudge — each piece hops a little way ( Nudge , at most) "},{"p":"Physics Tab","h":"Arrange","u":"physics-tab.html#arrange","x":"Tidy the selection up. Select objects (each placed piece moves on its own; a selected folder stands for the pieces in it) and pick a shape. Then press Space (or the button) and draw where they go in the Scene view — until then, clicks select as usual: Line — drag a line: they're spread evenly from one end to the other. Arc — drag from one end of the curve to the other; Bend is how much of a circle it is (180: a half circle). It bends to the left of the way you drag. Circle — drag from its middle out, or click its middle (the Radius ; 0 makes it just big enough). Grid — drag the rectangle they "},{"p":"Physics Tab","h":"Settle","u":"physics-tab.html#settle","x":"Under the shapes, for the selection as it stands (each placed piece on its own; a selected folder stands for the pieces in it): Drop lets them fall from where they are and settle on what's under them — tidy props that float a little, or tip a pile over. Untangle ( U ) pushes pieces that are inside each other apart and lifts ones sunk into the ground or a floor back on top — gently, with gravity off, so nothing flies. With Settle after untangling (on) they drop afterwards. One Undo step each. Both are in the Scene overlay too."},{"p":"Physics Tab","h":"Physics settings","u":"physics-tab.html#physics-settings","x":"Shared by every Physics tool (the Physics foldout): Bounce — how much things bounce when they land (0: not at all, 1: as high as they fell). Friction — how much they grip (0: they slide like on ice, 1: they stop where they land) — what they land on gets the same, so the setting decides. Keep upright — they stand up first (one that fell over stands again, facing the way it did) and never tip over, only turn round their up axis: furniture, trees, crates that should stand. In Place, Tumble then only turns them round. Time to settle — the longest they're given to come to rest, up to a minute (they"},{"p":"Dress Tab","h":"Dress Tab","u":"dress-tab.html#dress-tab","x":"Dressing what's built: furniture along the streets, closures across roads, signs with your text (on a board or flat on the ground), decals, cables, fences, variety (Vary), and blocks filled with buildings (Lots). Facades come in a later version. A park fenced with Dress > Fence, and street lights along every road. The Dress tools are laid out like the Place tools: the picked pieces at the top (a list with a picture of each — it scrolls on its own past three), the settings under them (they scroll on their own past eight lines), and the palette underneath. The mouse wheel scrolls whichever of th"},{"p":"Dress Tab","h":"Street","u":"dress-tab.html#street","x":"Your own kinds of street furniture along the sides of roads."},{"p":"Dress Tab","h":"Kinds","u":"dress-tab.html#kinds","x":"The panel lists your kinds of furniture — the rule set ships Street lights, Bins, Benches, Hydrants and Bus stops, with no pieces in them: kits name things too loosely to guess (a pile of trash is not a bin), so you pick them. At the top, every kind's pieces in one list : each with its picture and, under its name, a dropdown for its kind — change it to move the piece to another kind (with dressed roads selected, they're dressed again with it straight away). x takes a piece out. Under the list, one tab per kind — laid out as Closures' parts. The open tab shows that kind's settings, and palette "},{"p":"Dress Tab","h":"Dressing","u":"dress-tab.html#dressing","x":"Select road tiles — click them in the Scene view (Shift or Ctrl adds more) — and click Dress the selection (it counts them). Or drag a rectangle over roads in the Scene view. Ghosts show the furniture first. Let go (or click the button) to place it. One Undo step. Furniture goes on both sides of each road (alternate sides where the kind says so), fronts facing the road — never across a road's end, never within 2 m of a corner, never in front of a building's door. Each piece is a child of the road tile it stands by : move the tile and its furniture goes with it; delete the tile and it goes too."},{"p":"Dress Tab","h":"Closures","u":"dress-tab.html#closures","x":"A road closed off, in parts: barriers, cones, a sign, a wall and the road beyond. At the top, every part's pieces in one list : each with its picture and, under its name, a dropdown for the part it's for (Barriers, Cones, Sign, Wall, Road) — change it to move the piece to another part. x takes it out. Under the list, one tab per part — its settings, and the part palette clicks add pieces to (as many as you want, used in turn; click a piece again to take it out, or right-click a piece > Add to ): Tab Its pieces Its sliders Barriers a row across the road, centred on it Forward / back, Gap (betwe"},{"p":"Dress Tab","h":"Signs","u":"dress-tab.html#signs","x":"Signs with your text, placed where you click. Choose the object the text goes on in the palette — a sign board, preferably. It shows at the top, as the other tools' picked pieces do ( x , or clicking it again, goes back). With nothing chosen, a plain post-and-board sign is made. Set the Text (Enter for a new line), the Font (yours; left empty, a font that came with the sign's pack if it has one, else Unity's built-in font — no font is shipped), the Colour , the Size (how tall the letters are: Fit to the board fills the board, less a margin; moving Size sets it yourself instead) and where the t"},{"p":"Dress Tab","h":"Decals","u":"dress-tab.html#decals","x":"An image laid flat on any surface — graffiti, road markings and arrows, stains, posters, logos. Choose the Image : any texture in your project (a PNG with see-through parts works best). The images you used last show under it as buttons: a click chooses one again. Set its Width (m; its height follows the image's shape), Turn (°), Tint (white: as it is; a less opaque tint makes it fainter) and Brightness — decals aren't lit by the scene's lights, so lower it for one in the shade. Click the ground, a wall or a roof: the decal lies flat on it, reading the right way up from where you look (on a wal"},{"p":"Dress Tab","h":"Cables","u":"dress-tab.html#cables","x":"Cables hung between two points, sagging in the middle: power lines, phone lines, washing lines, bunting. Choose what it is: Wire (just the wire) or Bunting (little flags along it). Click where it starts — a pole, a wall, a roof, the ground — then where it ends : the cable hangs between. The clicks carry on from there, so clicking pole after pole runs a line down the street; Esc stops. A line shows where the next one will hang. With Hang from the top of poles (on by default) a click on a pole — a piece much taller than it's wide, a street light or a telegraph pole — hangs the cable from the pol"},{"p":"Dress Tab","h":"Fence","u":"dress-tab.html#fence","x":"Fence is in the Dress tab: fencing yards, lots and parks is part of dressing a street ( 4 in the Dress tab picks it). It picks its pieces from the palette the way the Place tools do (see Picking pieces ). Pick fence panels in the palette (several are used in turn). In Multiple , each picked piece has a dropdown — Panel , Post , Gate or Corner ; a gate (named \"gate\") or a post (named \"post\", or small and square) comes in as one. Right-click a piece for Add as … . Drag a rectangle in the Scene view (its corners snap to the pieces nearby and the grid, as switched; hold Ctrl for free corners) — or"},{"p":"Dress Tab","h":"Vary","u":"dress-tab.html#vary","x":"Make what's placed look less copied — a street of the same awning, the same house, the same crate. Vary only uses the variety your kits already have; a pack's own files are never changed. Piece — swaps it for a lookalike from its kit: the same category and about the same footprint and height, with a name of the same family (awning-red, awning-blue, awning-2; building-a, building-b). It stands where the old one stood. Material — swaps its materials for the pack's own alternatives of the same name family (Brick_01, Brick_02, Brick_Red), where the pack has them. Colour — tints each part with a pa"},{"p":"Fix Tab","h":"Fix Tab","u":"fix-tab.html#fix-tab","x":"Tools for what's already placed. Each works on the selection (or the scene) and is one Undo step."},{"p":"Fix Tab","h":"Ground","u":"fix-tab.html#ground","x":"Drops the selected objects straight down onto the first surface with a collider below them, so their bottom rests on it. Align to the slope off: their rotation is kept. On: each is turned to match the surface under it. A piece sunk into the ground comes up to its top (the surface is found from the top of the object down). Objects with nothing under them are left alone. Shortcut: Alt+Shift+D (works without the window open)."},{"p":"Fix Tab","h":"Floating","u":"fix-tab.html#floating","x":"Finds objects floating above, or sunk into, the surface under them. Choose Whole scene (every placed piece in the open scenes) or Selection . Set the tolerance : gaps smaller than it are fine (5 cm by default). Scan. Each object found is listed with its gap. - Click its name to select and frame it. - Fix drops that one onto its surface; Fix all drops every one (one Undo step). Shortcut: Alt+Shift+F opens this mode and scans."},{"p":"Fix Tab","h":"Doubles","u":"fix-tab.html#doubles","x":"Finds copies of the same piece stacked in the same spot — the same prefab, place, turn and size (a double click, a paste twice). They look like one piece, but draw twice and flicker. Pick Whole scene or Selection and click Scan : it says how many extra copies there are, and where. Select extras selects them, to look first; click a line to select one spot's copies and frame them. Remove extras keeps one copy in each spot (the first in the Hierarchy) and removes the rest, in one Undo step. Copies turned differently, or of a different size, aren't doubles and are never removed."},{"p":"Fix Tab","h":"Randomize","u":"fix-tab.html#randomize","x":"Jitters the selected objects, each at random within the ranges: Setting What it does Turn (±°) Turn about the up axis. Tilt (±°) Lean (0: stay upright). Scale Multiply each object's scale by a factor between the two values (1 – 1: no change). Colour Tint each part with a random colour of the active palette (Settings > Palettes) for its role — the walls a wall colour, the trim a trim colour. Any colour , or untick it and tick the colours to use (a part with none of its role ticked keeps its own). Seed The same seed gives the same result; the dice picks a new one. Colours are shared material var"},{"p":"Fix Tab","h":"Select","u":"fix-tab.html#select","x":"Selects every object like the selected one. Match Selects Same piece Every object placed from the same prefab. Same kit Every piece of the same kit. Same category Every piece of the same category. Same tag Every object with the same tag. Whole scene looks in every open scene; In view only at what the Scene view camera can see. Shortcut: Alt+Shift+S ."},{"p":"Fix Tab","h":"Replace","u":"fix-tab.html#replace","x":"Swaps placed objects for another piece. Pick the new piece in the palette (it shows under Replace with ). Select what to replace: click it in the Scene view (Shift or Ctrl adds more). Replace the selection: each one is swapped for the new piece, where it was — its place and turn, its own scale (a copy scaled by hand stays that much bigger or smaller) and its folder (a copy filed in its category's folder goes in the new piece's folder instead). The new piece's bottom middle goes where the old one's was, so pieces with different pivots neither float nor sink. Replace all swaps every copy of the "},{"p":"Check Tab","h":"Check Tab","u":"check-tab.html#check-tab","x":"Check what's placed: clipping, the rule set's rules, play from a spot, measuring and a performance readout."},{"p":"Check Tab","h":"Clipping","u":"check-tab.html#clipping","x":"Finds placed pieces that go into each other. Whole scene checks every placed piece in the open scenes (or the prefab being edited); Selection only what clips into the selected objects. Tolerance (cm) : overlaps thinner than this are fine. Pieces snapped flush only touch, so they never count. Left out: floors (what things stand on — Fix > Floating finds what's sunk into the ground), scatter , and the pieces of one fence, closure or spline (they meet by design). Count trees and plants adds trees and plants, whose crowns often meet. Where both pieces have colliders, their real shapes decide (an L"},{"p":"Check Tab","h":"Validate","u":"check-tab.html#validate","x":"Checks the scene (or the selection, measured against everything round it) against the rule set's rules — set in Settings > Rules , so nothing here assumes a game: Rule What's found Spacing Two pieces of a category closer than its smallest gap ( Min spacing ). Setback from the road A piece nearer the road than its category's smallest setback — or, with a road within 50 m, further than its largest. Triangle budget A piece over its category's triangle budget (once per piece, however many copies). Heavy scatter A scatter piece over the scatter budget: every copy counts. Crowded cell More props in "},{"p":"Check Tab","h":"Play","u":"check-tab.html#play","x":"Click a spot in the Scene view and Play starts there : the player — the first object with the Player tag (change the tag in the panel) — is put on the spot standing on it, facing the way the Scene view looks. With no player, the Main Camera goes there at eye height (from the Character Profile). Start Play (on): off, the player is only put there. Put back after playing (on): when Play ends, the player (or camera) goes back where it was. My game places the player : the kit moves nothing; your own spawn code reads the spot from AllInOneWorldBuilder.PlayFromHere — HasSpot , Position , Rotation — w"},{"p":"Check Tab","h":"Measure","u":"check-tab.html#measure","x":"Click points in the Scene view — two, or as many as you like along a path: every click adds a point, and each leg's length shows on its line (a dotted line shows the next leg to the mouse). The panel (and the Scene overlay) adds them up and says how long it takes to walk, run or drive — at the Character Profile's speeds (Settings > Rules) — and, when the points aren't level, how far it is along the ground and how far up or down . Backspace takes the last point off; Esc (or Clear ) starts over. Points land on anything under the mouse, models without colliders too."},{"p":"Check Tab","h":"Performance","u":"check-tab.html#performance","x":"What the whole scene — or only the selection — costs to draw, against the rule set's budgets (Settings > Rules > Performance): Objects and Triangles against their budgets: green, amber past three quarters, red over. A LOD group counts its most detailed level only. Materials (fewer means fewer draw calls) and Shadow casters (each is drawn again for the shadows). Heaviest : the meshes that cost the most triangles, with how many copies — click one to select them (Fix > Replace can swap them for something lighter). The counts update as the scene or the selection changes (and Count again counts now"},{"p":"Settings Tab","h":"Settings Tab","u":"settings-tab.html#settings-tab","x":"Everything you need is reachable here — no hunting through Project folders."},{"p":"Settings Tab","h":"Kits","u":"settings-tab.html#kits","x":"Every kit, with its piece count and style. Add Pack... opens the Add Pack wizard. Review... re-scans a kit's pack folders and opens the review screen (after a pack update, or to annotate pieces). Everything set by hand is kept. Click a kit's name to open its settings: rename it, change its style, and edit its settings (grid cell, scale, front side, placeable copies). After a change, Update the kit re-guesses what wasn't set by hand and rebuilds the placeable copies that changed. ⋮ : hide it from the palette (or show it again), merge another kit into it, export its annotations, remove it (optio"},{"p":"Settings Tab","h":"Styles","u":"settings-tab.html#styles","x":"Pick a style to rename it, set its colour and description, or delete it (its kits are left without a style). New makes one. The kits using it are listed."},{"p":"Settings Tab","h":"Rules","u":"settings-tab.html#rules","x":"Active rule set: the rules every tool follows. ⋮ : a new rule set with the defaults, duplicate this one, reset it to the shipped defaults , or delete it. Character profile: the character the world is built for (height, radius, step, slope, speeds), edited in place. New makes one. Rules: every rule of the active rule set, edited in place (see Rules, Profiles, Palettes and Styles ). Changes are saved at once. Tip: rule sets are data, so a newer Prefab Builder's improved default keywords don't reach an existing rule set on their own. Duplicate it to keep your changes, then Reset to defaults to pi"},{"p":"Settings Tab","h":"Palettes","u":"settings-tab.html#palettes","x":"The active palette and its colours (name, colour, role), edited in place. ⋮ : new, duplicate, delete."},{"p":"Settings Tab","h":"Folders","u":"settings-tab.html#folders","x":"The project settings: data folder, packs folder, the layer for small props and the tags for facade and enterable buildings, the default floor types and the floor colour tolerance, scene folders (root and one per category, floor type folders, piece folders, touching folders), never re-parent, pack detection. (Floor types themselves are in Floors & Terrain.) Tidy scene folders files every placed kit piece in the open scene again. If a named layer or tag isn't in the project, an Add button offers to create it — after asking. Nothing is created silently."},{"p":"Settings Tab","h":"Floors & Terrain","u":"settings-tab.html#floors--terrain","x":""},{"p":"Settings Tab","h":"Floor types","u":"settings-tab.html#floor-types","x":"Floor types are the kinds of ground — Grass, Asphalt, Concrete… — and the names Surface Tags carry. Every ground piece of your kits, and every Unity Terrain paint layer, is in one: Sorted automatically: by a word in its name first (\"road\", \"sidewalk\", \"grass\"); otherwise by its colour — the colour of its top, read from its texture at its own UVs (so kits sharing one palette texture sort correctly, and green tiles named only by number land in Grass). It joins the floor type of the nearest colour, or — if none is close (the floor colour tolerance in Settings > Folders) — a new floor type named a"},{"p":"Settings Tab","h":"Terrain","u":"settings-tab.html#terrain","x":"Terrain awareness — a switch that affects every tool: On: pieces follow Unity Terrain's height; pieces of the categories chosen under Follow the slope (nature, props, vehicles by default) also tilt to its slope; the floor type under a piece is the one of the terrain's dominant paint layer there (sorted like ground pieces: its name, else its texture's colour); the brush counts terrain as ground. Flatten under buildings: placing a building on terrain flattens the terrain under its footprint to its base, blending back into the slope over the blend margin . Flatten the terrain under the selection "},{"p":"Settings Tab","h":"Presets","u":"settings-tab.html#presets","x":"Export... and Import... open the Presets window (see Presets )."},{"p":"Settings Tab","h":"Hotkeys","u":"settings-tab.html#hotkeys","x":"Every Prefab Builder shortcut and its keys. Change , then press the new keys (Esc cancels). Reset puts the default back. They are Unity shortcuts: they also appear in Edit > Shortcuts . Unity's own Default profile can't be changed, so the first change asks to make a Prefab Builder shortcut profile (the defaults plus your changes) and switch to it. Reset all puts every Prefab Builder shortcut you changed back to its default keys (after asking; Unity's own shortcuts are left as they are). Save cheat sheet... writes every shortcut and mouse control to a text file to print. The keymap, with the mo"},{"p":"Rules, Profiles, Palettes and Styles","h":"Rules, Profiles, Palettes and Styles","u":"rules-and-data.html#rules-profiles-palettes-and-styles","x":"Everything the Prefab Builder decides is data you can change. Nothing assumes a particular game."},{"p":"Rules, Profiles, Palettes and Styles","h":"RuleSet","u":"rules-and-data.html#ruleset","x":"A RuleSet holds every placement rule. One is active at a time. The Default Rule Set is based on common city-building practice. Section What it holds Snap Default grid, Snap to pieces distance, rotation step (90°), height step, Snap to pieces on/off. Categories The categories pieces are sorted into (see below). Street furniture Your kinds of street furniture (Dress > Street): each with its picked pieces, density (per 100 m of road side), alternating sides and distance from the road edge. Closure types Road closed, bridge closed, private road: the sign texts Dress > Closures offers (plain data, "},{"p":"Rules, Profiles, Palettes and Styles","h":"Categories","u":"rules-and-data.html#categories","x":"Each category has an id (stored in kits: don't change it once used), a display name, and: Grid — the grid a piece snaps to when alone; 0 = the kit's grid cell (road and ground tiles). Setback min / max from the road edge, minimum spacing , triangle budget . Ground-like — its pieces get a Surface Tag and other pieces stand on them. Mark static , Add collider if missing — used when making placeable copies. Name keywords , folder keywords , exclude keywords — how the scanner recognises it. Keywords are comma separated. A word matches its plural, and keywords of 4+ letters also match longer words "},{"p":"Rules, Profiles, Palettes and Styles","h":"Character Profile","u":"rules-and-data.html#character-profile","x":"The character the world is built for: height (1.8 m by default), radius , step height , steepest slope , walk , run and vehicle speeds. Every scale rule reads from it (a doorway must be at least height + headroom), and travel-time estimates use its speeds. A RuleSet points to one profile."},{"p":"Rules, Profiles, Palettes and Styles","h":"Palettes","u":"rules-and-data.html#palettes","x":"Named colours (tints and accents), each with a role: wall, trim, accent, ground. Recolouring applies them through material variants of a kit's shared materials — one per colour, shared by every piece using it — never through unique materials per object. Two palettes are made on first use: Warm Town and Clean Suburb ."},{"p":"Rules, Profiles, Palettes and Styles","h":"Styles","u":"rules-and-data.html#styles","x":"A style is a look that kits belong to. It has a description and a colour (for lists and markers). The RuleSet's Style mixing rule uses it."},{"p":"Rules, Profiles, Palettes and Styles","h":"Project settings","u":"rules-and-data.html#project-settings","x":"Stored in ProjectSettings/AllInOneWorldBuilderSettings.asset : Setting Default Data folder Assets/AllInOnePrefabBuilderData Packs folder Assets/Imported Packs (where packs added from disk are copied) Small-props layer SmallProps Facade / enterable tags Facade , Enterable Floor types (surfaces) Asphalt, Concrete, Grass, Gravel, Dirt, Sand, Wood, Metal, Ceramic, Stone, Water — each with a colour and the words that suggest it (edited in Settings > Floors & Terrain) Floor colour tolerance 22 — how different a ground piece's colour may be and still join a floor type Default ground / road surface Co"},{"p":"Presets","h":"Presets","u":"presets.html#presets","x":"A preset is one plain JSON file holding any of: rule sets, character profiles, palettes, styles, kit annotations and project settings. Use presets to share a setup between projects or teammates, or to back it up. Assets refer to each other by name inside the file, and every part is optional. Open Tools > All In One Prefab Builder > Presets (Export, Import)..."},{"p":"Presets","h":"Export","u":"presets.html#export","x":"What: Everything ticked below — tick rule sets, character profiles, palettes, styles, kit annotations and settings. The active rule set and palette are remembered. What: Only the kit … — only that kit's settings and annotations. (Also on the Add Pack wizard's last page and in a kit's actions.) Kit annotations are the kit's settings and each piece's measurements and annotations. The pack's files are not in the preset: the project you import into needs the same pack."},{"p":"Presets","h":"Import","u":"presets.html#import","x":"Choose a file. A preview lists one line per asset: Create , Update (merge) or Replace , with details. Untick anything you don't want, then click Import . Merge — adds what's new; for what exists (same name), the file's values win and anything the file doesn't mention is kept. Lists inside a rule set are merged by id (categories, closure types, fill types), palettes by colour name. Replace — what exists with the same name becomes exactly what's in the file. For a kit, annotations the file doesn't have are cleared back to the scanner's guesses. Kit pieces are matched by GUID (the same pack impor"},{"p":"Presets","h":"The file","u":"presets.html#the-file","x":"<name>.wbpreset.json , readable and editable in any text editor. format and formatVersion identify it; a file from a newer version of the Prefab Builder is refused with a message rather than half-imported."},{"p":"Extending","h":"Extending","u":"extending.html#extending","x":"All code is in the AllInOneWorldBuilder (runtime) and AllInOneWorldBuilder.Editor (editor) namespaces, in the assemblies AllInOneWorldBuilder.Runtime and AllInOneWorldBuilder.Editor . Both are auto-referenced, so your own scripts can use them directly."},{"p":"Extending","h":"Surfaces","u":"extending.html#surfaces","x":"SurfaceTag (runtime) carries a surface name on an object; the Prefab Builder adds it to placeable copies of ground and road pieces. Read it from your game — the kit plays no sounds and assumes no footstep system: using AllInOneWorldBuilder; if (Physics.Raycast(foot.position, Vector3.down, out var hit, 0.5f) && SurfaceTag.TryGetSurface(hit, out var surface)) { PlayFootstep(surface); // \"Grass\", \"Asphalt\", ... as named in the Settings } Implement ISurfaceSource on your own components to supply surface names another way; SurfaceTag.TryGetSurface finds any ISurfaceSource on the object or above it."},{"p":"Extending","h":"Runtime components","u":"extending.html#runtime-components","x":"SurfaceTag / ISurfaceSource — above. LayerCullDistance — on a camera: stops it drawing one layer (the small-props layer scatter is on) past a distance. Uses Unity's per-layer cull distances; works in every render pipeline. ScatterChunk — marks a chunk of combined scatter (its group, cell, piece count). Plain data. WorldBuilderSpline — on a spline laid by the Spline mode: its points and layout settings, so it can be edited and laid again. Plain data; its pieces are its children. WorldBuilderMark — on what a tool made (street furniture, a closure): which tool and what for, so running the tool ag"},{"p":"Extending","h":"Data from code","u":"extending.html#data-from-code","x":"DataStore.ActiveRuleSet , DataStore.ActivePalette , DataStore.Kits() — the current data. Kit.Pieces — each KitPiece has its category, front side, measurements and LoadPrefab() . KitService — create, re-scan, rename, merge, hide and remove kits; KitService.KitsChanged fires after each. PresetService — export and import presets from code ( Export , ToJson , FromJson , Plan , Apply ). RoadGrid , StreetDresser , ClosureBuilder , SignBuilder — the Dress tab's: a road's cells and edges, street furniture planned and placed along them, a closure planned and built at a road end, text laid on a sign boa"},{"p":"Extending","h":"Your own modes and tabs","u":"extending.html#your-own-modes-and-tabs","x":"Subclass WorldBuilderMode (public parameterless constructor) in any editor assembly: the window finds it. Its Tab decides where it appears — one of WorldBuilderTabs (Move, Place, Physics, Dress, Fix, Check, Settings), or a name of your own, which becomes an extra tab after Settings. using AllInOneWorldBuilder.Editor; using UnityEditor; using UnityEngine; public class SpawnPointsMode : WorldBuilderMode { public override string Tab => \"My Game\"; public override GUIContent Label => new GUIContent(\"Spawns\", \"Place and check spawn points.\"); public override string Hint => \"Click to add a spawn poin"},{"p":"Extending","h":"Events","u":"extending.html#events","x":"WorldBuilderEvents (editor) tells you when the tools change the scene: Placed(GameObject placed, KitPiece piece) — a piece was placed (piece is null for a prefab that isn't in a kit). Changed(GameObject target, string what) — snapped, dropped to the ground… Removed(GameObject target) — raised before a tool removes an object. [InitializeOnLoadMethod] static void Hook() { WorldBuilderEvents.Placed -= OnPlaced; WorldBuilderEvents.Placed += OnPlaced; } static void OnPlaced(GameObject placed, KitPiece piece) { if (piece != null && piece.category == BuiltInCategories.Building) { /* add your own comp"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#troubleshooting","x":"Asking for help. In the Prefab Builder window, ⋮ > Copy support info puts what we need to know on the clipboard — the kit's version, Unity's, the render pipeline, and your kits (their names, piece counts and pack folders) — to paste into your message. Nothing is sent anywhere: it only goes where you paste it. A pack isn't offered on the first page. It needs at least 5 prefabs or models, must not already be a kit's folder, and must not be on the ignore list (\"Not a pack\"). Choose it in the folder browser instead. Pieces are in the wrong category. Fix them on the review screen (select several an"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Known limitations (this version)","u":"troubleshooting.html#known-limitations-this-version","x":"Place has Stamp, Brush, Row, Spline, Fill and Fence; Dress has Street, Closures and Signs. Rooms, Vary and facades, and the map builder and scene splitting come in later versions. The street dresser and closures work on the road grid (the road kit's cell): curved road pieces are dressed along their cells' straight edges. Sign text uses Unity's built-in text (TextMesh), which every render pipeline draws; TextMeshPro isn't used yet. Spline pieces are laid end to end along the curve: on a tight bend, straight pieces leave small gaps on the outside and overlap on the inside. Snapping uses visible "},{"p":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"shortcuts.html#keyboard-shortcuts","x":"Every Prefab Builder shortcut with its default keys. They are Unity shortcuts, so you can change any of them in Edit > Shortcuts (or the kit's own Settings > Hotkeys), and the tools follow your keys. Tool keys work while the mouse is over the Scene view or the kit's window."},{"p":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"General (anywhere in the editor)","u":"shortcuts.html#general-anywhere-in-the-editor","x":"Action Keys Open Prefab Builder Alt + Shift + W Toggle Snapping Alt + Shift + L Snap Selection Alt + Shift + G Snap Selection To Ground Alt + Shift + D Select Similar Alt + Shift + S Find Floating And Sunken Alt + Shift + F Quick Pick Alt + Shift + Q"},{"p":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"Any placing tool","u":"shortcuts.html#any-placing-tool","x":"Action Keys Straighten G"},{"p":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"Brush","u":"shortcuts.html#brush","x":"Action Keys Smaller [ Bigger ]"},{"p":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"Cutaway","u":"shortcuts.html#cutaway","x":"Action Keys Top Cut C Side Cut Shift + C"},{"p":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"Decals","u":"shortcuts.html#decals","x":"Action Keys Turn (press: one step; hold and move the mouse: freely) R Turn Back Shift + R"},{"p":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"Move","u":"shortcuts.html#move","x":"Action Keys Turn R Turn Back Shift + R Tilt T Tilt Back Shift + T"},{"p":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"Physics > Place (stamp)","u":"shortcuts.html#physics--place-stamp","x":"Action Keys Stamp Turn (press: one step; hold and move the mouse: freely) R Stamp Turn Back Shift + R Stamp Tilt (press: one step; hold and move the mouse: freely) T Stamp Tilt Back Shift + T"},{"p":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"Re-roll (after a Fill, Brush stroke, Row or Spline)","u":"shortcuts.html#re-roll-after-a-fill-brush-stroke-row-or-spline","x":"Action Keys Re-roll Last N Select Last Shift + N"},{"p":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"Row, Spline, Fill, Fence, Closures, Signs","u":"shortcuts.html#row-spline-fill-fence-closures-signs","x":"Action Keys Turn Fronts (press: one step; hold and move the mouse: freely) R"},{"p":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"Spline","u":"shortcuts.html#spline","x":"Action Keys Lay It Z Close Loop X Clear Backspace"},{"p":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"Stamp","u":"shortcuts.html#stamp","x":"Action Keys Turn (press: one step; hold and move the mouse: freely) R Turn Back Shift + R Tilt (press: one step; hold and move the mouse: freely) T Tilt Back Shift + T Grid Smaller [ Grid Bigger ] Toggle Place Over Red O"},{"p":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"Pick a tool","u":"shortcuts.html#pick-a-tool","x":"Action Keys Tool 1 of the open tab 1 Tool 2 of the open tab 2 Tool 3 of the open tab 3 Tool 4 of the open tab 4 Tool 5 of the open tab 5 Tool 6 of the open tab 6 Tool 7 of the open tab 7 Tool 8 of the open tab 8 Tool 9 of the open tab 9 Move > Move M Move > Select S Place > Stamp P Place > Brush B Physics > Arrange A"},{"p":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"Switch tabs","u":"shortcuts.html#switch-tabs","x":"Action Keys Tab 1 (in the order the tabs are shown) Shift + 1 Tab 2 (in the order the tabs are shown) Shift + 2 Tab 3 (in the order the tabs are shown) Shift + 3 Tab 4 (in the order the tabs are shown) Shift + 4 Tab 5 (in the order the tabs are shown) Shift + 5 Tab 6 (in the order the tabs are shown) Shift + 6 Tab 7 (in the order the tabs are shown) Shift + 7"},{"p":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"Mouse","u":"shortcuts.html#mouse","x":"Action How Stamp: place the piece Click Stamp: place even where the ghost is red Shift + Click Stamp: place freely (no snapping) Hold Ctrl (Cmd) Stamp, Row, Signs, Decals, Physics stamp: turn freely, any angle Hold R and move the mouse sideways Stamp, Physics stamp: tilt freely (its front down or up) Hold T and move the mouse sideways Pick up a placed piece (eyedropper) Alt + Click Drop a piece from the palette Drag it into the Scene view Brush: paint / erase Drag / Shift + Drag Brush: stay on the floor you start on Hold Ctrl (Cmd) while painting Row: lay a row (any angle) Drag (hold Ctrl) Spl"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Changelog","u":"changelog.html#changelog","x":"All notable changes to the All In One Prefab Builder Kit (called the All In One World Builder Kit up to 0.5.0). Versions follow Semantic Versioning ."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"[0.8.0] - 2026-10-10","u":"changelog.html#080---2026-10-10","x":"Updating: delete the old product folder ( Assets/AllInOnePrefabBuilderKit , or Assets/AllInOneWorldBuilderKit as it was called) before importing this version; your kits and settings are in the data folder and are kept. See the README."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Added","u":"changelog.html#added","x":"The Move tab (first; Alt+Shift+M): Move , Rotate and Scale what's placed with the kit's snapping instead of Unity's handles — 1, 2 and 3 switch between them (only there). Drag the object itself onto anything (a roof, a table); Move snaps to the pieces nearby and the grid on the axes dragged; Rotate turns round X, Y or Z in steps (R, Shift+R a step round the up axis), back onto the grid; Scale stretches by the box's faces (onto the grid) or evenly in steps by its corners, the bottom staying put. Ctrl moves freely. One Undo step per drag; works in Prefab Mode. Anchor for the Move tab — Point (cl"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Changed","u":"changelog.html#changed","x":"A keymap that makes sense (mouse over the Scene view or the window, window open): Shift+1–7 pick the tabs in order, 1–9 the tools of the tab that's open, and every tool has a key of its own straight to it from any tab — S Select, M Move, P Stamp, B Brush, A Arrange, the rest free to set. The keys lead the names on the buttons, greyed: \"⇧2 Place\", \"1 Stamp\". The old Alt+Shift tab and tool keys (Alt+Shift+1–5, M, H, B, R, J, K, I, E, X) are gone; Alt+Shift+W still opens the window. Settings > Hotkeys: Reset all puts every shortcut you changed back to its default keys. Every mode's help is one or"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Fixed","u":"changelog.html#fixed","x":"Place > Roads: the straight stretches use the tile named for it (road-straight) — before, a tile as open but painted with a zebra crossing (road-crossing) could be picked, and every stretch of road had a crossing on it. The sample kit's street lights face the way their arm reaches, so Dress > Street hangs their lamps over the road (their arms pointed at the houses). The showcase scenes (Showcase City, Try It) are rebuilt: plain roads, lights over the road, room between the buildings, nothing clipping, and grass out to the horizon. Row: starting the drag no longer turns the pieces away from the"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"[0.7.0] - 2026-09-30","u":"changelog.html#070---2026-09-30","x":"Quick wins, a sample kit and a demo town to try them on."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Added","u":"changelog.html#added_1","x":"Sample kit: Kenney's City Kits (Commercial, Industrial, Roads, Suburban — CC0) ship in Samples/ ; one click (the empty palette, the tour, or ⋮ > Add the sample kit) makes them one kit, sorted, at one scale on a 10 m grid. Demo City ( Samples/Demo ): a small town built from the sample models — roads with crossings, a fenced park, shops, houses, towers, industry, street lights, a road closure and woodland on a grass terrain — to try every tool on. Fix > Replace: pick the new piece in the palette and swap the selected objects for it — or every copy of a piece (Replace all) — each where the old on"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Changed","u":"changelog.html#changed_1","x":"A piece dragged from the palette into the Scene view follows the grid and snaps to the pieces nearby while you drag (its ghost, as in Stamp), and lands exactly there — before, Unity dropped it freely and it was snapped afterwards. The scanner tells URP and HDRP apart: a URP material in an HDRP project (or the other way round) is flagged as pink too. The scanner measures which way a road piece's lanes run (from its raised kerbs) and faces it along them: Stamp's arrow points where the next piece of road goes, and Row's \"along\" lays road tiles lanes-first. To update a kit, Review it (Settings > K"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Fixed","u":"changelog.html#fixed_1","x":"Esc: Unity won't let Escape be a shortcut, so the kit's three Esc shortcuts were ignored (with a warning in the Console) and Esc didn't cancel a Row, Fill or Fence drag. The tools now read the key itself: Esc cancels, puts Stamp's piece down, or hands the Scene view back to Unity's tools. No more warning. The Brush painted nothing near pieces placed straight from a pack's model and scaled by hand (as in Demo City): it sized them with the kit's scale twice (a 2 m tree counted as 100 m across), so every spot looked crowded. It now sizes what's standing by what it covers in the scene. \"Follow the"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"[0.6.0] - 2026-09-29","u":"changelog.html#060---2026-09-29","x":"A round of improvements to every Place and Dress tool, the palette and the window, from early-access feedback."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Added","u":"changelog.html#added_2","x":"Renamed: the All In One Prefab Builder Kit. The window is the Prefab Builder , its menu Tools > All In One Prefab Builder , its shortcuts under \"All In One Prefab Builder\" (shortcuts you changed need setting again). Your data folder, kits and settings are unchanged. A cleaner window: flat tabs, each tool in titled cards (its pieces, its settings with Grouping in a section of its own, the palette with a count), a two-row palette filter bar, soft tiles behind the pictures, and one notice bar look for what's selected, renaming and new categories. Fill: Area / Selection / Inverse. Selection fills "},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Changed","u":"changelog.html#changed_2","x":"Snap to pieces (was Lego snap) and the grid are separate switches — either, both (snapping to a piece wins) or neither. Snap to pieces puts a piece flush against the pieces near it and lines it up along that side as well as it can; flat tiles snap side by side (a road brought up to a road lies right beside it); a piece takes the turn of what it snaps to; with Place over red on, a piece pushed into another still lines up with it. The Flush, \"snap dropped pieces\" and \"snap after moving\" options are gone. The grid is the same for every tool that places things (Stamp, Brush, Row, Spline, Fill, Fen"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Fixed","u":"changelog.html#fixed_2","x":"A sky dome or skybox object round the scene (250 m or more every way) no longer turns every ghost red. Stay on this floor sees ground and road pieces without colliders. Random order and the pieces' own scale work in Row and Spline. A short, wide road stub has its real end as its end (closures and Street); Street dresses a single road tile. Cones and barriers stand centred on the road's lanes, level with the road."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"[0.5.0] - 2026-09-26","u":"changelog.html#050---2026-09-26","x":"Stage 4: the Dress tab — Street, Closures, Signs. The first early-access upload."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Added","u":"changelog.html#added_3","x":"Dress > Street: your own kinds of street furniture (the rule set ships Street lights, Bins, Benches, Hydrants and Bus stops, empty), each with its pieces, distance from the road edge, density and alternating sides. Select road tiles and dress the selection, or drag over roads: ghosts first, fronts to the road, clear of corners, road ends and doorways. Each piece becomes a child of its road tile; dressing again replaces what the dresser placed; Clear takes it away. Dress > Closures: barriers, cones, a sign, a wall and the road carrying on, each part with its pieces, sliders and quarter turns, a"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Changed","u":"changelog.html#changed_3","x":"Road pieces file in a Roads folder inside their floor type ( World/Ground/Asphalt/Roads ); closures in a Closures folder inside it. Tidy scene folders leaves a closure's parts inside the closure. Esc with nothing to cancel hands the Scene view to Unity's tools (it used to go back to Stamp)."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"[0.4.0] - 2026-09-26","u":"changelog.html#040---2026-09-26","x":"Stage 3: Row, Spline, Fill, Fence."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Added","u":"changelog.html#added_4","x":"Place > Row: drag to lay pieces in a straight line — end to end with a gap, or one every so many metres — fronts facing one side (front edges on the line) or along it; several pieces in turn or at random; a cap for each end; Count; spread to fit; follow the ground; stay on this floor; pieces really standing in something show red (ground, roads and sidewalks never count) and are left out unless Place over red — one switch shared with Stamp — is on. The row starts on the first piece's ghost exactly where it stood when pressed (Lego-snapped) — pressing never moves or turns it; the drag sets its d"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Changed","u":"changelog.html#changed_4","x":"Tidy scene folders leaves a spline's pieces inside the spline."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Removed","u":"changelog.html#removed","x":"Fill types from the rule set (Fill fills with the pieces you pick instead)."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"[0.3.0] - 2026-09-26","u":"changelog.html#030---2026-09-26","x":"Stage 2: brush, combine, randomize, terrain."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Added","u":"changelog.html#added_5","x":"Place > Brush: pieces picked in the palette with weights and kinds; drag to paint to a density with random turn and scale; Shift-drag erases; [ and ] resize; only on the ground; tilt to slope. One Undo per stroke. Brush: Stay on this floor (a switch, or hold Ctrl): a stroke only paints on the floor type it starts on, never spilling onto roads or pavement. Floor types grouped by colour: ground pieces (the colour of their top, read from the texture at their own UVs) and terrain paint layers join the floor type of the nearest colour when no word in their name decides — or a new one named after th"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Removed","u":"changelog.html#removed_1","x":"Ground-type rules (\"category X only on floor type Y\", shipped with trees only on grass): Stamp and Brush never refuse a piece because of the floor under it. Stay on this floor replaces them for keeping the brush where it belongs."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Fixed","u":"changelog.html#fixed_3","x":"The first scatter copy of a kit failed when its folder didn't exist yet (caught by the new tests)."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"[0.2.0] - 2026-09-26","u":"changelog.html#020---2026-09-26","x":"Stage 1: the World Builder window."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Added","u":"changelog.html#added_6","x":"The World Builder window ( Tools > All In One World Builder > World Builder , Alt+Shift+W): tabs Place, Dress, Fix, Check, Settings, Advanced; a mode selector per tab; help per mode; an overflow menu; packs in the project that aren't kits yet offered at the top. A Scene view overlay with the active mode's essentials and a one-line hint. Place > palette: every piece of every visible kit, favourites first, recent pieces, search, kit and category filters; click to make active, drag into the scene, right-click to favourite. Place > Stamp: a ghost preview (green fits, red overlaps or breaks a groun"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"[0.1.0] - 2026-09-26","u":"changelog.html#010---2026-09-26","x":"Stage 0: the foundation."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Added","u":"changelog.html#added_7","x":"Data assets: Kit, Style, RuleSet (every placement rule as data), Character Profile, Palette; project settings (layers, tags, surfaces, scene folders, pack detection) stored in ProjectSettings/ . A default RuleSet based on common city-building practice, a standard 1.8 m Character Profile and two palettes, made in your data folder the first time they're needed. Pack scanner: finds every prefab and model in a pack (models already wrapped by the pack's prefabs are not counted twice), measures footprint, height, triangles (LOD 0), materials and pivot, and flags problems (empty material slots, shade"}];
